# dotfiles-Arch/.gitattributes — line-ending policy for the OS-native layer.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS SEPARATELY FROM core/.gitattributes:
# gitattributes are DIRECTORY-SCOPED. The vendored core/.gitattributes governs
# core/** and nothing else, so every file THIS repo owns — bootstrap.sh, os/*.zsh,
# os/*.conf, os/*.gitconfig, install/packages.txt, ssh/config, wsl/wsl.conf — was
# left to git's defaults. On a clone made from Windows (this repo lives on ext4 and
# is reachable over \\wsl.localhost, where a Windows git reports core.autocrlf=true)
# that means CRLF can be written into the working tree.
#
# The failure is the one core/.gitattributes already documents, one layer out: a
# CRLF bootstrap.sh carries a trailing CR on its shebang line and dies with
# "bad interpreter: No such file or directory". A CRLF ssh/config or wsl.conf is
# worse — both parse, but the trailing \r lands INSIDE the values (an ssh
# ControlPath with a \r, a WSL default user that does not exist), so they fail at
# runtime rather than at load. None of it is visible to shellcheck or to the
# lint gate: the files are valid text.
#
# NOTE (companion rule, not enforceable here): do git WRITE operations on this repo
# from inside WSL, not from Windows over the UNC share. gitattributes fixes line
# endings; it does not govern the executable bit, and a Windows-side git reads the
# share as mode 0644 and would strip +x from bootstrap.sh and from every script
# under core/ — which Core's audit asserts, and which changes HEAD:core's tree
# object, so core-integrity.yml would then red every PR. See CLAUDE.md.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Default: normalize all detected-text files to LF in the index and on checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ── Executable + sourced shell — MUST stay LF or the shebang/sourcing breaks ───
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.zsh       text eol=lf

# ── The OS-native surface: parsed by ssh, WSL, tmux, git and pacman at runtime,
#    where a stray \r is a silent wrong VALUE rather than a parse error. ────────
ssh/config          text eol=lf
wsl/wsl.conf        text eol=lf
*.conf              text eol=lf
*.gitconfig         text eol=lf
install/*.txt       text eol=lf
core.lock           text eol=lf

# ── Repo meta ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.md        text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.jsonc     text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
Makefile    text eol=lf
LICENSE     text eol=lf

# ── Binaries (defensive): never normalize, never diff as text ─────────────────
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.gz        binary
*.zip       binary
